.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml

# ── Misc / secrets (never commit) ───────────────────────────────
*.log
.env
# Oura BYO-app OAuth credentials (never committed)
Strand/Oura/OuraSecrets.xcconfig
# Deployment credentials (PAT / signing / endpoints) — never commit. `.env` above
# won't match `deploy.env`, so list it (and any `deploy.env.<stage>`) explicitly.
deploy.env
deploy.env.*
secrets.*
android/local.properties
android/keystore.properties
android/noop-release.jks

# ── Personal data — NEVER publish (real WHOOP/Apple Health exports) ──
Fixtures/
*.sqlite
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm
export.xml
export.zip
*whoop_export*.zip
*apple_health*.zip

# Python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# ── Rust (liters dependency) ────────────────────────────────────
# Build output only. Rust/Cargo.lock IS tracked: it pins the exact liters
# commit this tree builds against, which a branch-tracking git dependency
# otherwise leaves floating.
Rust/target/

# Local release artifacts (zips/ipas built for upload; never tracked)
dist/
err.txt

# Oura OAuth app credentials (BYO; never committed)
Strand/Oura/OuraSecrets.xcconfig

# Personal bundle id prefix (BYO; never committed)
Config/BundleIdSecrets.xcconfig

# liters linkage, written by Rust/build-ios.sh from the xcframework it just produced. Untracked
# because it names slice directories under Rust/target/, which is itself untracked — committing it
# would point every other checkout at an archive that is not there. Config/Liters.xcconfig is the
# tracked wrapper that optionally includes it.
Config/LitersLocal.xcconfig

# (#979) The linux-capture workbench moved from `tools/` to `Tools/` when the two case-colliding
# trees were merged. On a case-SENSITIVE filesystem any capture a contributor already had stays
# behind at the old path, where that tool's own .gitignore no longer reaches it — and those files
# carry HR and timeline, while the .db carries the strap serial, session token and MAC.
#
# These are DATA patterns only, never the bare directory. Git folds case in .gitignore when
# core.ignorecase is on (which it is on macOS), so `tools/linux-capture/` would also match the REAL
# `Tools/linux-capture/` and silently ignore every new file added there — a new test suite would
# vanish, which is the exact failure this whole normalisation exists to prevent. Every pattern below
# is one that Tools/linux-capture/.gitignore already applies, so folding onto the real tree is a
# no-op there while the orphaned old path stays covered here.
tools/linux-capture/*.json
tools/linux-capture/*.db
tools/linux-capture/*.db-wal
tools/linux-capture/*.db-shm
tools/linux-capture/captures/
